-- infra/e2e/seed/70-phase4-batch1-inbox-fixture.sql.tmpl
--
-- Phase 4 batch 1 (mig-infra) — data-point + cursor-pagination parity
-- fixture for the read-only /inbox surface (conversationList's
-- /api/inbox/conversations + /api/inbox/messages Route Handlers, messageThread's
-- /inbox + /inbox/[userId] pages). Applied AFTER 30-/40-/60-phase2-*.sql.tmpl
-- (already piped into the SAME app/tenant DB by parity.mjs's seedDatabase())
-- — additive only, same pattern those files already established. Does not
-- touch any table an earlier batch already seeds rows into.
--
-- No USE statement of its own — same convention as every fixture before it:
-- the caller (parity.mjs's seedDatabase()) prepends `USE \`<app-db>\`;`.
--
-- DETERMINISM: every timestamp is a MySQL relative-date expression
-- (NOW()/DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL n MINUTE|SECOND)), never a wall-clock
-- literal — same reasoning as every earlier fixture's own header comment.
-- Within ONE multi-row INSERT statement MySQL evaluates NOW() exactly once
-- and reuses that same instant for every row, so the relative MINUTE/SECOND
-- offsets below are self-consistent regardless of how long the statement
-- itself takes to execute.
--
-- LINE_ACCOUNT_ID = 1 ON PURPOSE (unlike every earlier fixture's users rows,
-- which are all line_account_id IS NULL — see 30-phase2-batch1-fixture's own
-- "CURRENT-BOT-ID NOTE"): api/inbox/conversations/_lib/query.ts's
-- accountConditionExpr() scopes the LINE-platform list with
-- `u.line_account_id = accountId` where accountId = session.currentBotId ??
-- 1 — an EQUALITY test, not the NULL-tolerant `(line_account_id = ? OR
-- line_account_id IS NULL)` pattern users.php/groups.php/etc. use. Since
-- this harness's Next tenant session currentBotId is ALWAYS null (no
-- line_accounts rows are ever seeded here — see that same note), accountId
-- always resolves to the literal 1 on the NEXT side, and every earlier
-- batch's users row (line_account_id IS NULL) therefore does NOT match `= 1`
-- and is correctly excluded from the LINE inbox list there. Setting
-- line_account_id = 1 on every conversation user below is what makes them
-- show up on the NEXT side, and — just as importantly — makes the TOTAL
-- conversation count returned by the conversations-cursor-walk EXACTLY the
-- number of users this file seeds (no earlier batch's users can leak into
-- the count).
--
-- PHP-SIDE ASYMMETRY (confirmed by this batch's own harness run — see
-- infra/e2e/parity.mjs's runInboxSidebarChecks() and
-- docs/runbooks/phase4-batch1-inbox-reads-parity.md for the full trace):
-- inbox-v2.php's OWN `$currentBotId = $_SESSION['current_bot_id'] ?? 1`
-- (line 81) gets UNCONDITIONALLY CLOBBERED to `NULL` by
-- `includes/header.php` (line 174, `$currentBotId = $currentBot['id'] ??
-- null`) before the conversation-list query ever runs, because both files
-- share one top-level-include PHP scope and there are no accessible
-- `line_accounts` rows for header.php to resolve a bot from. `u.line_account_id
-- = NULL` matches zero rows on the PHP side, regardless of what this file
-- seeds — a real, pre-existing PHP defect (any freshly-provisioned tenant
-- with no LINE OA connected yet would see a permanently empty inbox
-- sidebar), not something this fixture can or should work around. This is
-- exactly why `/inbox`'s harness entries are two single-side assertions,
-- not a PHP-vs-Next diff, for the sidebar specifically — the thread page
-- (`?user=N` / `/inbox/N`, section 3 below) is UNAFFECTED (its queries never
-- filter by `line_account_id` at all) and IS diffed normally.
--
-- CONVERSATION LAYOUT (215 total conversations = users with >=1 messages row):
--   7001              — HERO: the messageThread golden dataset. 130 messages
--                    spanning every message type/form this batch's brief
--                    lists (see section 3 below for the full inventory).
--                    Most recent conversation (last message at NOW(), i.e.
--                    0 SECOND ago) — always first in the DESC-sorted list on
--                    both stacks. Zero unread (deliberately — inbox-v2.php's
--                    `?user=N` page load has a mark-as-read side effect this
--                    port's /inbox/[userId] deliberately does NOT reproduce
--                    yet (see that page's own module doc); giving HERO
--                    unread messages would make a PHP-side page-pair fetch
--                    mutate is_read out from under a later JSON assertion on
--                    the very same conversation. See the runbook's "why
--                    HERO has zero unread" note.
--   7002              — badge satellite: tagged with user_tags.id=1 ('VIP',
--                    seeded by 30-phase2-batch1-fixture.sql.tmpl — tag
--                    lookups have no line_account_id filter at all, see
--                    api/inbox/conversations/_lib/query.ts's
--                    getUserTagsBatch(), so reusing that tag is safe).
--                    Exercises ConversationRow.tags + the sidebar's
--                    `data-tags="1"` attribute.
--   7003              — badge satellite: one `conversation_assignments` row
--                    (assigned_to=1, status='active'). Exercises
--                    ConversationRow.assigned_to/assignment_status — NOT
--                    rendered as visible sidebar markup (inbox-v2.php's
--                    per-row template never reads `conversation_assignments`
--                    directly, only `conversation_multi_assignees` — see
--                    7004 below), so this is a JSON-API-only assertion.
--   7004              — badge satellite: one `conversation_multi_assignees` row
--                    (admin_id=1, status='active'). Exercises
--                    ConversationRow.assignees + the sidebar's
--                    `data-assigned="1"` attribute + the "มอบหมายแล้ว" badge.
--   7005              — badge satellite: users.chat_status = 'pending'. Exercises
--                    ConversationRow.chat_status + the sidebar's
--                    `data-chat-status="pending"` attribute + the 🔴 badge.
--   7006              — badge satellite: 2 unread incoming + 1 read outgoing
--                    message (unread_count = 2). Exercises
--                    ConversationRow.unread_count + the sidebar's
--                    `unread-badge` element. NEVER visited via a PHP
--                    `?user=` page-pair fetch by this harness (see HERO's
--                    own note above for why that would be a mutation
--                    hazard) — only read via the JSON conversations-cursor
--                    walk, which never mutates anything.
--   7007-7215 — 209 filler conversations, one plain incoming text
--                    message each, is_read=1, no tags/assignment. Pure
--                    volume for the cursor-pagination walk (this batch's
--                    brief requires >=210 total conversations; 215
--                    comfortably clears that with margin). Spaced 1 MINUTE
--                    apart (offsets 6..214 minutes ago) so every
--                    conversation in this file has a strictly distinct
--                    last_message_at — required for a deterministic
--                    ORDER BY last_time DESC on both stacks (ties would make
--                    cursor-boundary assertions flaky). HERO + the 5 badge
--                    satellites use SECOND/MINUTE offsets 0..5 minutes ago,
--                    so the full ordering is unambiguous:
--                    7001 (0s) > 7002 (1m) > 7003 (2m) > 7004 (3m) > 7005 (4m) > 7006 (5m) > 7007..7215 (6m..214m).
--                    With the SSR sidebar capped at 200 rows on both stacks
--                    (see api/inbox/conversations/_lib/query.ts's own
--                    "ARCHITECTURE NOTE"), the oldest 15 of these
--                    fillers never appear in the /inbox / /inbox-v2.php
--                    page-pair fetch at all — by design, the same way real
--                    production behaves once a tenant has more than 200
--                    active conversations. They are still reachable, and
--                    still counted, via the conversations-cursor-walk
--                    (which pages all the way to has_more=false).

